      <p class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-[0.18em] uppercase text-gray-400 mb-4">Landscaping · Getting customers</p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl mb-6 leading-tight">How to get more landscaping customers, <span style="color:var(--primary)">ranked by payback</span></h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">Seven channels that work for landscaping and lawn care companies, in the order we would actually try them. Two of them are free and fast, and the one most companies reach for first is the one we would try last.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="py-20 lg:py-28 bg-white">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5">

    <div class="max-w-3xl mb-16">
      <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-5">The order below is deliberate. It runs from cheapest and fastest to most expensive and slowest, because that is the order that keeps a company solvent while it builds something durable. Skipping to number six because it sounds like real marketing is how landscaping companies end up paying a retainer for twelve months before anything happens.</p>
      <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">One thing to settle first: know what a customer is worth to you. A weekly mow at $45 that renews for three years is worth around $7,000. A one-off cleanup is worth $300. If you do not know which one a channel brings, you cannot tell whether it is working.</p>
    </div>

    <ol class="space-y-6 mb-20 list-none pl-0">

      <li class="rounded-2xl border border-gray-100 p-7 bg-white shadow-sm reveal">
        <p class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-widest uppercase mb-2" style="color:var(--primary)">01 · Free · Pays back this week</p>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3 text-gray-900">The customers you already have</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-3">Unglamorous and consistently the highest return. Two moves: ask every satisfied customer whether a neighbor needs the same service, and look at what each account is <em>not</em> buying from you. A customer on weekly mowing who pays someone else for mulch, irrigation repair or palm trimming is money already sitting in your route.</p>
        <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">Raising an existing account from $45 a week to $70 costs you nothing in drive time. Winning a new $45 account somewhere else costs you the drive, the estimate, and the risk.</p>
      </li>

      <li class="rounded-2xl border border-gray-100 p-7 bg-white shadow-sm reveal" style="transition-delay:.05s">
        <p class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-widest uppercase mb-2" style="color:var(--primary)">02 · Nearly free · Pays back in days</p>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3 text-gray-900">The street you are already parked on</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-3">Route density is the whole economics of this business. While the crew is working a property, someone leaves a card on the six nearest doors: we are working at number 14 today, here is what we charge for a yard this size.</p>
        <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">It converts far better than generic flyers because the proof is right there — they can see the truck and the finished lawn. And every account it wins lands on a street you already drive to.</p>
      </li>

      <li class="rounded-2xl border border-gray-100 p-7 bg-white shadow-sm reveal" style="transition-delay:.10s">
        <p class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-widest uppercase mb-2" style="color:var(--primary)">03 · Free · Pays back in 60–90 days</p>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3 text-gray-900">Google Business Profile</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-3">The three businesses in the map get most of the calls, and claiming and working the profile costs nothing but attention. For a lot of landscaping companies this is the single best move available, and most have either never verified it or set it up once in 2019 and never touched it again.</p>
        <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">The catch is that Google ranks you by distance from your verified address, so it wins you your own area and not the county. <a href="/en/landscaping/local-seo/" style="color:var(--primary)">How the map pack actually works →</a></p>
      </li>

      <li class="rounded-2xl border border-gray-100 p-7 bg-white shadow-sm reveal" style="transition-delay:.15s">
        <p class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-widest uppercase mb-2" style="color:var(--primary)">04 · Free · Compounds forever</p>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3 text-gray-900">A review habit, not a review push</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-3">Reviews decide both whether Google shows you and whether the person calls once it does. The mistake is treating it as a campaign: forty requests in one week, then nothing for a year. Google weighs recency, so a steady trickle beats a burst.</p>
        <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">Build the ask into how a job closes rather than remembering to do it. And a review that says "re-sodded our back yard in Vero Beach" is worth several that just say "great job" — you can ask for that without scripting anyone.</p>
      </li>

      <li class="rounded-2xl border border-gray-100 p-7 bg-white shadow-sm reveal" style="transition-delay:.20s">
        <p class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-widest uppercase mb-2" style="color:var(--primary)">05 · Free · Pays back in weeks</p>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3 text-gray-900">Neighborhood groups and Nextdoor</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-3">"Anyone know a good landscaper?" gets posted in local Facebook groups constantly, and the answer is whoever a neighbor remembers. Being the name that comes up is worth more than any ad, and it costs you ten minutes a day of actually being present.</p>
        <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">Post finished work, not offers. Nobody in a neighborhood group wants a promotion, but everyone stops for a dramatic before-and-after from three streets over. If your area has a large Spanish-speaking community, posting in both languages roughly doubles the reach at no extra cost.</p>
      </li>

      <li class="rounded-2xl border border-gray-100 p-7 bg-white shadow-sm reveal" style="transition-delay:.25s">
        <p class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-widest uppercase mb-2" style="color:var(--primary)">06 · Costs time · Pays back in 3–6 months</p>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3 text-gray-900">Your website and local SEO</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-3">This is the one that builds an asset. Pages for the services you want more of and the neighborhoods the map will not reach, earning calls every month without paying per click. It is also genuinely slow — the first quarter is groundwork.</p>
        <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">Worth it if you plan to still be running this company in three years. Not the right first move if you need work in March.</p>
      </li>

      <li class="rounded-2xl border border-gray-100 p-7 bg-white shadow-sm reveal" style="transition-delay:.30s">
        <p class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-widest uppercase mb-2" style="color:var(--primary)">07 · Costs money · Pays back immediately</p>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3 text-gray-900">Google Ads and Local Services Ads</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-3">The fastest channel and the only one you can switch on tomorrow. It is also the only one that stops the moment you stop paying. Current market rates: a click on a landscaping search runs around $10, a lawn care lead closer to $19, and clicks on "google ads for landscapers" style searches around $29.</p>
        <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">Do the arithmetic before you start. At $19 a lead and a one-in-four close rate, a customer costs you $76. Against a $7,000 recurring account that is excellent. Against a $300 one-off cleanup it is thin. Run it deliberately for the work with real lifetime value, not as a general tap.</p>
      </li>

    </ol>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mb-20">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-6">The one we would skip</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-5">Lead-selling platforms — the ones that charge per lead and sell the same homeowner to four companies at once. They will fill your phone, and you will spend your evenings racing three competitors to the bottom on price for a customer who was shopping on cost from the first click.</p>
      <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">Some companies make them work as a way to fill genuinely empty weeks. As a foundation they train you to compete on price, which is the one competition a small landscaping company cannot win.</p>
    </div>

